// A `...base` spread whose type is `dyn` leaves the set of fields it contributes open: which slots
// it fills is a property of the value, and there is no value until the program runs. So the
// completeness rule (E0009) stays with the backends for this literal, and both raise it at
// construction time with the same code, span and wording the checker uses where it can decide
// statically (`literal_missing_field_static.noe`).
//
// `alive` is printed before the construction, so the expectations say the program ran. A checker
// that rejected this literal instead would print nothing, and the stdout line below would fail —
// which is what keeps the two halves of the rule from quietly becoming one.
// expect: stdout "alive"
// expect: error E0009 at 27:6
// expect: exit 1
struct Point {
    x: int
    y: int
}

struct Flat {
    x: int
}

fn opaque(): dyn {
    return Flat { x: 1 };
}

echo "alive";
echo Point { ...opaque() }.y;
